  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Struck out nine times and scored once. That's the Padres' reality right now.",
    "what_this_means": "Michael King delivered six clean innings, allowed one run, and still the offense couldn't back him. They trailed after the second, tied it up, then spent the rest of the night chasing traffic that never turned into runs. Two runners in scoring position and nothing to show for it. This is not execution under pressure. This is execution failing when it matters most.",
    "what_to_watch": "The formula doesn't work when the bats are this quiet. King did his job perfectly. The pitching has been reliable all season, sitting at 4.15 ERA with a staff that knows how to survive. But survival requires the lineup to convert something. Nine strikeouts tells you everything: they're swinging at bad things, missing good ones, and leaving the whole game to chance. Against St. Louis, at home, with a lead they couldn't keep. The margin between 22-15 and 22-16 is one swing they didn't take.",
